Carmelo Lombardo has managed this  seafood shop in Sydney for three decades  and is used to seeing thousands of  people pass through its doors each week  now there's only a handful and he's had  to adapt to survive the business is  still open but most of its sales are  taking place online instead with staff  delivering produce to homes those casual  staff that we used to utilize in in a in  the retail shop we've then moved them  around to suit the online other  businesses in the Sydney fish market  aren't so lucky  some have closed others have seen  revenues drop by up to 80% within our  retail tendencies certainly there's been  stand downs and other job losses which  is really unfortunate Australia isn't in  lockdown but strict social distancing  measures main shopping strips remain for  the most part empty with doors shut and  many out of work but keeping people at  home seems to be working  despite some outbreaks Australia's  infection rate has slowed recording  fewer than 100 new cases a day well down  from weeks ago and it's had a relatively  low death rate with the economic costs  rising business groups are pushing for  restrictions to start being lifted and  there are petitions from local people  who want to return to beaches any  prospect of lifting restrictions is a  delicate balance for the government at  this stage it says it's too early to  ease any social distancing measures  worried that it could lead to a second  wave of cases similar to other countries  like Singapore and Japan infectious  disease experts agree but if  restrictions were to be lifted they say  it would have to happen slowly with  international borders remaining closed  for some time start to lift some of the  restrictions locally until that happens  businesses are navigating a new normal  as the government works on how and when  life can return to what it was before  the crisis Nikola gage al Jazeera Sydney
 

